 ## Overview
-Analytical gradients compute the derivatives of the volume rendering equation with respect to input parameters (such as density, geometry, or camera pose) by explicitly expanding the chain rule, rather than relying on automatic differentiation (AD) or finite differences. This approach solves the problem of high memory consumption and computational overhead associated with reverse-mode AD, which requires storing $O(kn)$ intermediate states for $n$ pixels and $k$ samples [S5]. By deriving closed-form expressions for the gradients, this family reduces memory usage to $O(n)$ and enables efficient optimization of dynamic volumetric densities and geometric parameters [S1, S5].
+Analytical gradients compute the derivatives of the rendering equation with respect to input parameters (such as density, geometry, or camera pose) by explicitly expanding the chain rule, rather than relying on automatic differentiation or finite differences [G]. This approach solves the problem of high memory consumption and computational overhead associated with reverse-mode AD, which requires storing $O(kn)$ intermediate states for $n$ pixels and $k$ samples [G]. By deriving closed-form expressions for the gradients, this family reduces memory usage to $O(n)$ and enables efficient optimization of dynamic volumetric densities and geometric parameters [G]. It sets itself apart from [Automatic Differentiation (Reverse/Adjoint Mode)](7151-automatic-differentiation-reverse-adjoint-mode.md) by avoiding computational graph storage, and from [Differentiable Volume Rendering via Box Splines](2449-differentiable-volume-rendering-via-box-splines.md) and [Analytical Derivative of Rendering (Mesh)](6761-analytical-derivative-of-rendering-mesh.md) by providing the explicit mathematical formulation for the gradient computation itself.
 
 ## Shared principles
-The core mechanism relies on the differentiability of the continuous components of the rendering pipeline (projection, shading, or emission-absorption integration) while acknowledging that discrete steps (such as triangle selection or voxel traversal order) are non-differentiable [S1, unofficial].
+Analytical gradients compute derivatives by explicitly expanding the chain rule across the continuous stages of the rendering pipeline (projection, integration, or shading), bypassing the computational graph storage required by reverse-mode AD [G]. For volumetric methods, the rendering integral is differentiated term-by-term, yielding a direct term for local emission and an indirect term for downstream transmittance or visibility attenuation [G]. For mesh-based methods, gradients flow through differentiable projection matrices, barycentric interpolation, and shading models, while discrete visibility selection remains non-differentiable [G]. All implementations maintain $O(n)$ memory complexity relative to the output size, enabling single-pass or tightly coupled backward passes [G].
 
